      "authorID": null,
      "boardID": "58c62f2b3cf8f8367753b2f0",
      "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
      "created": "2026-08-26T12:27:25.200Z",
      "deleted": false,
      "deletedAt": null,
      "fileURLs": [],
      "files": [],
      "imageURLs": [
        "https://canny-assets.io/images/b70c2ddee27a2152705430101670415c.png",
        "https://canny-assets.io/images/3304f7829d2e410390ab4e6aeecbb16b.png"
      ],
      "integrationSourceType": null,
      "internal": false,
      "itemSourceType": null,
      "mentionedUserIDs": null,
      "mentionedUsers": [],
      "mergeID": null,
      "parentID": null,
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "69e1109614916839a1a48a59",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": null,
      "restoredAt": null,
      "sourceFeatureExtractionItemID": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "The game leaks even on safe mode. It's not someone's avatar. No asset bundles were even loaded because I've had the game in safe mode to eliminate that variable. Yet I still encounter OOM crashes quite frequently. However, I managed to catch this one at 51GB and a minute or two after forcing full GC with the debug panel, the excess leaking heap was released.\n\nI'm guessing the mono managed heap starts leaking during some allocation event and fails to release its unused heap pages during normal garbage collection. So this isn't a traditional memory leak caused by something related to Unity. Unity's memory usage didn't change. It's VRChat itself.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
    {
      "_id": "6a914a179c0d859282516e11",
      "aiGenerated": false,
      "author": {
        "_id": "5cb6a908df8d8e58c38318a3",
        "alias": "Static Badger",
        "aliasID": "65fc43eba63714bcb32ec335",
        "anonymized": false,
        "avatarURL": "https://canny-assets.io/images/9b80423d29b5e6307dd5eb80411c9380.png",
        "created": "2019-04-17T04:18:16.853Z",
        "name": "Graystripe",
        "serviceAccount": false,
        "urlName": null
      },
      "authorID": null,
      "boardID": "58c62f2b3cf8f8367753b2f0",
      "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
      "created": "2026-08-28T08:43:03.951Z",
      "deleted": false,
      "deletedAt": null,
      "fileURLs": [],
      "files": [],
      "imageURLs": [],
      "integrationSourceType": null,
      "internal": false,
      "itemSourceType": null,
      "mentionedUserIDs": null,
      "mentionedUsers": [],
      "mergeID": null,
      "parentID": null,
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "69e1109614916839a1a48a59",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": null,
      "restoredAt": null,
      "sourceFeatureExtractionItemID": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "I just caught another leak, and this time I paid attention to the steps I took before the leaking memory was actually released to confirm what I thought happened last time in the post below this one, and the behavior was exactly the same.\n\nFor some reason, forcing full GC in the debug menu does not actually release the leaked memory. Instead, what I have to do to reclaim it is open the debug menu, force full GC, close it, reopen it, force full GC again, close it again, and by the third or fourth time opening the debug menu the RAM usage will drop. The large initial drop seems to be triggered by opening the debug menu on that third or fourth time, and then from there I can force full GC to clean up the remaining several GB and return VRChat's memory usage to normal.\n\nNow I don't know if the leak is causing some circular reference that doesn't let GC take care of it until it's forced a few times or what's going on, but simply pressing the force full GC button will not resolve the leak. You have to do it a few times, close the debug menu, reopen it, etc before the memory is released. I figured mentioning this is probably useful if the devs are even reading this.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
